+# Encounter consistency decision addendum — proposed revision 62
+
+**Status: proposed; not applied or adopted in this checkout.** This proposed addendum supersedes revision 59 only for reference arm length, measured-dimension priority, and the throwing equation denominator. Prior decision text remains unchanged as historical provenance.
+
+## Reference arm length and Reach terminology
+
+- Native Being stature/length and weapon length are inches. For positive measured height or length, use `L_inches = (15/37) × max(height_inches, length_inches)`; measurements take precedence over category.
+- If neither dimension is positive, use an explicitly known category estimate: Diminutive 3.5, Tiny 7, Small 14, Medium 28, Large 56, Huge 112, Gargantuan 224, Colossal 448 inches. Do not infer category from geometry or an unimplemented category stub; do not add individual random variation.
+- Reach is a scalar used in spatial comparisons, never a world coordinate. This does not change the separate adopted melee center-distance rule.
+
+## Throwing equation and preserved constraints
+
+For `x = projectile_weight_lb / heavy_load_reference_lb`, retain `Vmax_yd_s = 5 × (Strength + Throw_skill_modifier − 2)/2`, where the modifier is +1 for any acquired Throw level and 0 otherwise. Calculate:
+
+`Vrelease_yd_s = Vmax_yd_s × exp(−(x / 0.08081)^0.6010) × sqrt(L_inches / 28 inches)`.
+
+The 28-inch denominator makes the Medium reference factor exactly one. The heavy-load value remains a numerical normalizer, not a physical causal law. Preserve the separate maximum-throwable-weight constraint and the minimum 5 yd/s effective-adjacent-hex threshold. Throw velocity remains yd/s; a future trajectory boundary must explicitly convert to m/s (×0.9144) while spatial coordinates remain meters.
+
+The revision-59 0.800 m Medium anchor and metric series are historical/superseded for current calculation. Any interim 30-inch reference is likewise historical/superseded; keep old revision records unchanged.
+
+This does not create a native Throw Action or integrate the helper with Encounter scheduling. Maximum-weight tables, projectile trajectory integration, and any additional eligibility rule remain separate.
